module assemblyQC
module transformationQC

manifest VectorBackbone
manifest Promoter1
manifest BCD1_gfp
manifest T4_DNA_Ligase
manifest HFE7500_PluronicF127
manifest AssembledPlasmid
manifest ElectroporationMedia
manifest LB_Recovery
manifest CompetentEcoli

// ============================================================
// A Versatile Microfluidic Device for Automating Synthetic Biology
// Shih, Goyal, Kim, Koutsoubelis, Keasling, Adams, Hillson, Singh
// ACS Synthetic Biology 2015, 4, 1151-1164
// DOI: 10.1021/acssynbio.5b00062
//
// Platform: Hybrid digital-to-droplet microfluidic device
// (75 x 25 mm; 76 DMF electrodes + PDMS serpentine channel).
// Three functional regions: DNA assembly (DMF), queuing and
// incubation (serpentine channel), transformation (electroporation
// region with microelectrodes).
//
// ASSEMBLY METHOD ENCODED: Golden Gate (primary, Fig 6 in paper).
// Golden Gate is the representative method because it is the only
// one with a full 16-plasmid sequencing panel shown in the main
// text. Gibson (50 C, 15 min) and Yeast/TAR (room temp, 10 min,
// no ligase) follow the same droplet operations with different
// incubation parameters; separate .bs files should encode those.
//
// REAGENT VOLUMES:
//   Reservoir droplets: 1.5 uL (dispensed onto reservoirs).
//   Dispensed subdroplets: ~0.2 uL per mix step on DMF.
//   Total DNA assembly volume: ~0.8 uL (Golden Gate / Gibson)
//                               ~0.6 uL (Yeast).
//   T4 DNA ligase: 2.5 U/uL with 0.05% w/v Pluronic F-127.
//   All DNA fragments: 30-40 ng/uL, gel-purified after DpnI
//   and BsaI digest.
//
// CROSS-CONTAMINATION STRATEGY: HFE 7500 biocompatible oil
// filler + 0.05% w/v Pluronic F-127 surfactant additive
// (instead of wash steps) prevents biofouling. This is the
// first application of this strategy to on-chip DNA assembly.
// Modelled here by dispensing HFE7500_PluronicF127 as the
// continuous oil phase that surrounds all DMF droplets.
//
// GOLDEN GATE INCUBATION:
//   Temperature: 25 C (room temperature).
//   Duration: 10 min.
//   BsaI-HF cuts at Type IIS sites; T4 ligase joins overhangs.
//   Paper Table 1: total volume 0.8 uL, 2 DNA fragment insertions.
//
// ELECTROPORATION:
//   Target: E. coli DH5alpha electrocompetent cells.
//   Media: 8 mM Na2HPO4, 2 mM KH2PO4, 250 mM sucrose pH 7.4.
//   Pulses: 3 x 100 ms DC pulses at 1800 V/cm.
//   Maximum efficiency: 4.58e3 CFU/ng assembled DNA (Fig 5a).
//   Recovery: 200 uL LB broth, 1 h at 37 C before plating.
//   Two bare gold microelectrodes (2.0 x 0.3 mm), 100 um gap.
//   Oil phase (HFE 7500) prevents electrical short when no
//   droplet is present; impedance feedback triggers pulses.
//   Electroporation is modelled as a heat step at 37 C (the
//   post-pulse recovery temperature; the pulse itself is
//   instantaneous and has no BioScript duration token).
//
// INCUBATION CHANNEL:
//   Serpentine PDMS channel, 1.5 mm wide, stores 16 droplets
//   for > 2 h without volume loss. Peltier heater enables
//   region-specific temperature control (heating only the
//   incubation zone, leaving reservoirs at room temperature
//   to preserve T4 ligase activity). Modelled via heat.
//
// ONE-REACTION ABSTRACTION:
//   This .bs file encodes one representative Golden Gate
//   assembly reaction (pProm1_BCD1-gfp, the canonical test
//   plasmid, ~3070 bp). The actual chip runs 16 combinatorial
//   reactions (4 Promoter variants x 4 BCD variants) in
//   sequence using the same electrode path and incubation
//   channel. Encoding all 16 in BioScript would require 16
//   independent reagent manifests for each promoter/BCD
//   variant; a single canonical reaction captures the full
//   protocol logic without misrepresenting the paper.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- device init; reagent dispensing;
//                           DNA fragment mixing; T4 ligase
//                           addition; digital-to-droplet
//                           transfer; incubation; detect
//   BB2  assemblyPass     -- assemblyQC > 0: assembly succeeded;
//                           dispose assembled plasmid droplet
//   BB3  assemblyFail     -- assemblyQC <= 0: assembly failed;
//                           drain assembled plasmid droplet
//   BB4  assemblyJoin     -- join: transformation branch begins
//   BB5  transformPass    -- transformSignal > 0: colonies
//                           detected; dispose recovery droplet
//   BB6  transformFail    -- transformSignal <= 0: no colonies;
//                           drain recovery droplet
//   BB7  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose QC signal droplets
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Prime oil phase.
//   HFE 7500 + 2% Pluronic F-127 surfactant is pipetted
//   onto the DMF surface (1 mL). The oil phase surrounds
//   all aqueous droplets, preventing cross-contamination
//   and biofouling without additional wash steps.
//   Modelled as a single dispense representing the bulk
//   oil-phase load. This oil droplet is referenced during
//   later digital-to-droplet transfer steps.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

oilPhase = dispense 1 units of HFE7500_PluronicF127

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Dispense vector backbone droplet (~0.2 uL).
//   Reservoir: 1.5 uL of pFAB4876-based vector backbone
//   at 30-40 ng/uL. Vector concentrations matched to
//   BCD/Promoter insert concentrations by vacuum concentrator
//   (65 C, 5-10 min) prior to chip loading.
//   BsaI and DpnI digested, gel-purified.
//   Golden Gate vector backbone: ~2100 bp band (Fig 3a).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

vectorDrop = dispense 1 units of VectorBackbone

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Dispense Promoter1 insert droplet (~0.2 uL).
//   Source plasmid: pFAB4876 (Promoter1, ~100 bp band).
//   DpnI + BsaI digested; gel-purified; 30-40 ng/uL.
//   Four promoter variants used across 16 reactions:
//   Prom1, Prom2, Prom9, Prom11.
//   This .bs file encodes the Prom1 variant.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

promoterDrop = dispense 1 units of Promoter1

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Dispense BCD1-gfp insert droplet (~0.2 uL).
//   Source plasmid: pFAB4876 (BCD1_gfp variant, ~800 bp).
//   DpnI + BsaI digested; gel-purified; 30-40 ng/uL.
//   Four BCD variants: BCD1, BCD2, BCD20, BCD21.
//   This .bs file encodes the BCD1 variant.
//   The gfp gene enables colony color screening (green =
//   correct insert, white = self-ligation artifact).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bcdDrop = dispense 1 units of BCD1_gfp

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Dispense T4 DNA ligase droplet (~0.2 uL).
//   T4 DNA ligase at 2.5 U/uL with 0.05% w/v Pluronic F-127.
//   Pluronic additive prevents biofouling without inhibiting
//   ligation activity (Au et al., Langmuir 2011).
//   IMPORTANT: T4 ligase reservoir stays at room temperature
//   while incubation zone is heated (region-specific Peltier).
//   This prevents heat inactivation (T4 denatured above 50 C)
//   before the ligase droplet is dispensed.
//   For Gibson assembly, this step uses 2x Gibson master mix
//   instead; for Yeast assembly, no ligase droplet is needed.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ligaseDrop = dispense 1 units of T4_DNA_Ligase

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Merge DNA fragments.
//   Vector backbone + Promoter1 insert mixed on-chip at
//   160 V_rms, 15 kHz via electrode actuation. Droplet
//   circulates 2-3 times over mixing electrodes (1.75 x 1.75 mm
//   digital-to-channel electrodes near D2D interface).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dnaMix = mix vectorDrop with promoterDrop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Add BCD1-gfp insert to DNA mix.
//   Three-component droplet now contains all DNA parts for
//   one Golden Gate reaction. Total merged volume: ~0.6 uL.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

threePartMix = mix dnaMix with bcdDrop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Add T4 DNA ligase to complete the Golden Gate mix.
//   Final droplet: ~0.8 uL containing vector, Promoter1,
//   BCD1-gfp, and T4 DNA ligase (2.5 U/uL).
//   Mixed 2-3 times in circular fashion on mixing electrodes.
//   This merged droplet is ready for digital-to-droplet
//   transfer into the incubation channel.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

assemblyMix = mix threePartMix with ligaseDrop for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Digital-to-droplet transfer and incubation.
//   The syringe-vacuum pump (0.1 uL/s) draws the assembled
//   droplet from the DMF surface into the 1.5 mm wide
//   serpentine PDMS channel. Oil phase (HFE 7500) at 5 uL/s
//   drives the droplet into the incubation zone.
//   Golden Gate incubation: 10 min at 25 C (room temperature).
//   BsaI-HF digest creates overhangs; T4 ligase joins them.
//   Paper Table 1: 10 min incubation, room temperature.
//   The heat step at 25 C models the Peltier-controlled
//   region-specific temperature maintenance during incubation.
//   (For Gibson: 50 C, 15 min. For Yeast: 25 C, 10 min.)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat assemblyMix at 25c for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Assembly QC detection.
//   assemblyQC module abstracts an on-chip or off-chip
//   verification step. In the paper, plasmid assembly is
//   confirmed by colony color (green GFP fluorescence)
//   after electroporation and plating. On-chip, no direct
//   assembly verification is performed before electroporation;
//   the detect step here models the GFP/colony screening QC
//   as a logical gate to decide whether to proceed to
//   transformation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

assemblySignal = detect assemblyQC on assemblyMix for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Assembly QC gate
// Pass (BB2): assemblySignal > 0 -> Golden Gate ligation
//             succeeded (expected ~95% correct clones post-seq);
//             dispose assembled droplet and proceed.
// Fail (BB3): assemblySignal <= 0 -> ligation failed (e.g.
//             self-ligation white colony dominant); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (assemblyJoin).
// ============================================================

if (assemblySignal > 0) {
    dispose assemblyMix
} else {
    drain assemblyMix
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: assemblyJoin
// Dispose assembly QC signal. Control falls into the
// electroporation / transformation branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose assemblySignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Dispense E. coli cells for electroporation.
//   DH5alpha electrocompetent cells dispensed from the cell
//   inlet into the electroporation region.
//   Electroporation media: 8 mM Na2HPO4, 2 mM KH2PO4,
//   250 mM sucrose, pH 7.4 (prevents osmotic lysis).
//   Cell droplet dispensed by applying potential to 4
//   microelectrodes (three 1.4 x 0.7 mm + one 4.0 x 0.7 mm)
//   near the cell-inlet/main-channel intersection.
//   Elastomeric microvalve (valve 5, Fig S2) prevents
//   cross-contamination between successive plasmid droplets.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cellDroplet = dispense 1 units of CompetentEcoli

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12: Dispense incubated plasmid exiting the channel.
//   After 10 min Golden Gate incubation in the serpentine
//   channel, the assembled pProm1_BCD1-gfp droplet is driven
//   by oil phase (HFE 7500, 5 uL/s) into the electroporation
//   region. In BioScript ownership terms, the incubated
//   assembly product is a new fluid object at this stage:
//   assemblyMix was consumed by detect (assemblyQC) in BB1;
//   incubatedPlasmid models the same physical droplet as it
//   exits the incubation channel and enters the electroporation
//   zone. This avoids the assemblyMix2 phantom SSA index
//   emitted by the compiler when a variable is re-used after
//   detect has already consumed it.
//   Oil phase replaces cell-inlet flow; all channel valves
//   closed (see Fig S2 for valve sequence).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

incubatedPlasmid = dispense 1 units of AssembledPlasmid

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13a: Mix cells with electroporation media.
//   Cell droplet resuspended in electroporation media on-chip
//   before merging with the plasmid droplet.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

electroporationMedia = dispense 1 units of ElectroporationMedia
cellMix = mix cellDroplet with electroporationMedia for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13b: Merge incubated plasmid with cell mix.
//   The incubated plasmid droplet and the cell+media droplet
//   merge in the electroporation region immediately upstream
//   of the two bare gold microelectrodes (2.0 x 0.3 mm,
//   100 um gap). Total merged volume: ~2 uL.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

preMix = mix incubatedPlasmid with cellMix for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 14: Electroporation.
//   3 x 100 ms DC square pulses at 1800 V/cm applied to the
//   merged droplet via two bare gold microelectrodes
//   (2.0 x 0.3 mm, 100 um gap, no dielectric coating).
//   Impedance-sensing feedback (Vsense threshold ~0.15 V)
//   detects droplet on electrodes and triggers pulse delivery.
//   Continuous oil phase prevents electrical short when
//   no droplet bridges the electrode gap.
//   Maximum efficiency: 4.58e3 CFU/ng (110 +/- 8 colonies,
//   N=3, comparable to cuvette: 4.46e3 CFU/ng, Fig 5a).
//   Modelled as heat at 37 C (post-pulse recovery temperature;
//   the pulse itself is instantaneous).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat preMix at 37c for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 14: Recovery.
//   Electroporated droplet driven to outlet by oil phase
//   (HFE 7500, all valves closed). Collected in tube
//   containing 200 uL LB broth. Incubated 1 h at 37 C
//   (standard outgrowth for kanamycin-selected DH5alpha).
//   50 ug/mL kanamycin selection plates used for plating.
//   ~100 of ~110 colonies show green color (GFP+, correct
//   insert); ~10 white (self-ligation artifacts).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

recoveryMedia = dispense 1 units of LB_Recovery
recoveredCells = mix preMix with recoveryMedia for 5s

heat recoveredCells at 37c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 15: Transformation QC detection.
//   transformationQC module abstracts off-chip colony count
//   on kanamycin LB-agar plates (37 C, overnight incubation).
//   Colony-PCR and Sanger sequencing (QB3284_fwd / QB3810_rev)
//   confirm correct assembly in 95% of green colonies (Fig 6,
//   76/80 correct for Golden Gate; 80/80 for Gibson).
//   On-chip proxy: impedance-sensing detects viable cell
//   density in the electroporated droplet as a QC signal.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

transformSignal = detect transformationQC on recoveredCells for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Transformation QC gate
// Pass (BB5): transformSignal > 0 -> transformed colonies
//             detected; dispose recovery droplet.
// Fail (BB6): transformSignal <= 0 -> no viable transformed
//             cells (electroporation failure or no assembly);
//             drain recovery droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (transformSignal > 0) {
    dispose recoveredCells
} else {
    drain recoveredCells
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose transformation QC signal and oil phase droplet.
// Experiment ends. In the actual protocol, this step is
// repeated 15 more times (16 total reactions) using the
// same electrode path, mixing channel, and incubation
// channel to generate all 4 Prom x 4 BCD combinatorial
// plasmid variants. Each successive iteration uses fresh
// reservoir droplets refilled via the SU-8 capillary.
// ============================================================

dispose transformSignal
dispose oilPhase

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
